e per kind.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:33:#      across different keys, so the connector match is necessary and visually supported.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:51:#    - Repeated body colors across different groups make it visible that body color alone\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:82:    - Count the non-singleton sprites in that connector's cluster, then paint that many evenly spaced dots across the legend row using the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/1/code.py:46:#     - Humans can compare lane heights directly across 2-4 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/1/code.py:171:    Key insight: The move is no longer announced by special marker colors; the solver must infer source and target roles by comparing visible stack heights across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D16/0/code.py:62:  - Across 2-4 examples, the human-visible difference is that the marker stands alone instead of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/1/code.py:44:#   excessive clutter and to preserve repeated evidence across frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/22/code.py:123:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D10/1/code.py:57:#       of the bars\" is stable across all examples and easy to read.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:35:#    never touch each other, and never cross the classifier boundary used by the solver.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:45:#    a separate pool and intentionally repeat across both halves so the human solver can reject color matching.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:252:    # Guarantee at least one repeated color across both halves, so color alone cannot explain the tally rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D12/2/code.py:61:#   - 